<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Taylor Series and its convergence</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The main objectives of this section are to answer the following questions:
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Under what conditions, the function <m> f(x) </m> is equal to the Taylor series generated 
            by <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>? That is, when can we say 
            <m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m>, <m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k</m> , is used as an 
            approximation to <m>f(x)</m>, how can we estimate the error in this approximation? 
            That is, how can we estimate the magnitude of <m>
            R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

  </objectives>
  <p>
    <xref ref="Taylor-Formula"/> and <xref ref="thm-remainder-theorem"/> will enable us to answer these questions.
    We will begin by stating Taylor's formula and the remainder estimation theorem, and then we will discuss some applications of these theorems.
    The proof of the remainder theorem is postponed to the end of this section because it requires some technical details that are not necessary 
    for understanding the main ideas of this section. 
  </p>

   <subsection xml:id="subsection-1">
    <title> Taylor's Formula and The Remainder Estimation Theorem </title>
    <theorem xml:id="Taylor-Formula">
      <title>Taylor's Formula</title>
      
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval 
          <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number 
          <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
          <me>
            f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
          </me>
          where 
          <me>
            p_n(x)= \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
          </me>
          and
        <me>
          R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
        </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
  <figure xml:id="fig-taylor-formula">
  <caption>Taylor's Formula: <m>f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x)</m>. The Taylor
  polynomial <m>p_n</m> agrees with <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>; away from
  <m>a</m>, the vertical gap between them is the remainder
  <m>R_n(x)</m>.</caption>
  <image width="65%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="taylor-formula">
      <diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="8">
        <!-- Schematic for Taylor's Formula: f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x).
             f and its Taylor polynomial p_n agree (and are tangent) at x = a;
             they separate as x moves away, the vertical gap being R_n(x). -->
        <definition>a = 1</definition>
        <definition>f(x) = 0.9 + 0.55*sin(1.15*(x - 0.4)) + 0.09*x</definition>
        <definition>pn(x) = f(a) + 0.5778*(x - a) - 0.379*(x - a)^2</definition>
        <definition>xt = 3.1</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-0.4, -0.3, 4.1, 2.3)">
          <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
          <graph at="fgraph" function="f" domain="(-0.2, 3.9)"
                 stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
          <graph at="pngraph" function="pn" domain="(-0.2, 3.9)"
                 stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
          <group at="tangency">
            <point p="(a, f(a))" fill="black" size="3"/>
            <line p1="(a, 0)" p2="(a, f(a))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(a, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-2)"><m>a</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="remainder">
            <line p1="(xt, 0)" p2="(xt, f(xt))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(xt, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-2)"><m>x</m></label>
            <point p="(xt, f(xt))" fill="blue" size="3"/>
            <point p="(xt, pn(xt))" fill="red" size="3"/>
            <line p1="(xt, pn(xt))" p2="(xt, f(xt))"
                  stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
            <label anchor="(xt, pn(xt))" alignment="sw" offset="(-8,-14)">
              <m>R_n(x)</m>
            </label>
          </group>
          <label at="flabel" anchor="(3.55, f(3.55))" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)">
            <m>f(x)</m>
          </label>
          <label at="pnlabel" anchor="(3.5, pn(3.5))" alignment="e" offset="(6,-2)">
            <m>p_n(x)</m>
          </label>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="fgraph"
              text="A schematic illustration of Taylor's Formula, f of x equals p n of x plus R n of x. A function f and its Taylor polynomial p n agree at x equals a and separate as x moves away, the gap being the remainder">
            <annotation ref="fgraph"
                text="The graph of the function f of x, shown in blue"
                sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="pngraph"
                text="The graph of the Taylor polynomial p n of x, shown in red, which is tangent to f at x equals a and closely matches f near a"
                sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="tangency"
                text="The point x equals a, where f and its Taylor polynomial p n agree; the approximation is exact here"/>
            <annotation ref="remainder"
                text="At a point x away from a, the vertical gap between f and p n is the remainder R n of x, the error in the Taylor approximation"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof of the remainder theorem is based on the mean value theorem and Taylor's theorem and we have included 
        the proof in <xref ref="subsection-5"/>. The proof is postponed to the end of this section because 
        it requires some technical details that are not necessary for understanding the main ideas of this section. 
        <alert> The students are not expected to know the proof, however, they should understand the statement and its 
          implications. Interested students are encouraged to read the proof.</alert> 
      </p>
    </proof>
    <remark>
  <title>Finding an upper bound for the error term without knowing the value of <m>c</m></title>
  <p>
  Usually the value of <m>c</m> is not explicitly known. However, we may manage to find <alert>an upper bound</alert> 
   for the error term <m>R_n(x)</m> without knowing the exact value of <m>c</m>. This is achieved by finding an upper bound <m> M</m>
   for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m>, where <m>c</m> is between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, and then using this upper bound to estimate the error term.
  </p>
</remark>

 <theorem xml:id="thm-remainder-theorem">
      <title>The Remainder Estimation Theorem</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval 
          <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number 
          <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
          <me>
            |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!} |x-a|^{n+1},
          </me>
          where <m>M</m> is an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof of the remainder estimation theorem is based on the Taylor's formula <xref ref="Taylor-Formula" />. 
        Suppose that we can find an upper bound <m>M</m> for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, i.e., <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)| \leq M</m>, then:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_n(x)| = \left| \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1} \right| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!} |x-a|^{n+1}.
      </me> 
    </proof>


      


  </subsection>
 
    <subsection xml:id="subsection-2">
    <title>An example of a Taylor series that converges</title>
    <p>
      Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. We know that <m>f^{(n)}(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>n\geq 0</m>. 
      Therefore, the Taylor series generated by <m>f</m> at <m>x=0</m> is given by:
    </p> 
    <me>
      \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!} x^n = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n!} x^n
    </me>
    <p>
      We will show that this series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x\in \R</m>. To do this, we will use the remainder theorem. We have:
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1} = \frac{e^c}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      First, we consider the case when <m>x>0</m>. Since <m>c</m> is between <m>0</m> and <m>x</m>, 
      we have <m>0 \leq c \leq x</m>. Therefore, we can bound the remainder term as follows:    
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) \leq \frac{e^x}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      Now we can compute the limit of the remainder term as <m>n\to \infty</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \lim_{n\to \infty} |R_n(x)| \leq \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{e^x}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1} = 0
    </me>
    <p>
      This shows that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x>0</m>. 
    </p>

   <figure xml:id="fig-exp-taylor">
  <caption>Schematic graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and its Taylor polynomials
  <m>p_1, \dots, p_4</m> about <m>0</m>. For a positive <m>x</m> the remainder
  uses some <m>c</m> with <m>0 &lt; c &lt; x</m>, giving <m>e^c &lt; e^x</m>;
  for a negative <m>x</m> it uses some <m>c</m> with <m>x &lt; c &lt; 0</m>,
  giving <m>e^c &lt; 1</m>.</caption>
  <image width="80%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="exp-taylor">
      <diagram dimensions="(440,360)" margins="8">
        <!-- Schematic: e^x with Taylor polynomials p1..p4 about 0.
             No axis numbers; dashed markers show a representative x>0 with an
             intermediate c (0<c<x), and a representative x<0 with c (x<c<0). -->
        <definition>f(x) = exp(x)</definition>
        <definition>p1(x) = 1 + x</definition>
        <definition>p2(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2</definition>
        <definition>p3(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6</definition>
        <definition>p4(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-2.7, -1.9, 2.25, 8)">
          <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
          <graph at="p1" function="p1" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p2" function="p2" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="green" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p3" function="p3" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="purple" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p4" function="p4" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="fgraph" function="f" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
          <label at="flabel" anchor="(-1.55, 4.4)" alignment="w" color="blue">
            <m>f(x) = e^x</m>
          </label>
          <point at="center" p="(0, 1)" fill="black" size="2.5"/>
          <group at="pos-x">
            <line p1="(0.8, 0)" p2="(0.8, f(0.8))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.8, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(6,-2)"><m>x \gt 0</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="pos-sample">
            <line p1="(0.5, 0)" p2="(0.5, f(0.5))" dash="4 4" stroke="olive" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(-4,-2)" color="olive"><m>c</m></label>
            <point p="(0.5, f(0.5))" fill="olive" size="4"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5, f(0.5))" alignment="n" offset="(-4,16)" color="olive">
              <m>e^c \mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} e^x</m>
            </label>
          </group>
          <group at="neg-x">
            <line p1="(-0.8, 0)" p2="(-0.8, f(-0.8))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(-0.8, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(-6,-2)"><m>x \mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m></label>
          </group>
          <group at="neg-sample">
            <line p1="(-0.5, 0)" p2="(-0.5, f(-0.5))" dash="4 4" stroke="maroon" thickness="1"/>
            <label anchor="(-0.5, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(4,-2)" color="maroon"><m>c</m></label>
            <point p="(-0.5, f(-0.5))" fill="maroon" size="4"/>
            <label anchor="(-0.5, f(-0.5))" alignment="n" offset="(-8,16)" color="maroon">
              <m>e^c \mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 1</m>
            </label>
          </group>
          <legend at="legend" anchor="(-2.5, 7.7)" alignment="se" scale="0.85">
            <item ref="fgraph"><m>e^x</m></item>
            <item ref="p4"><m>p_4</m></item>
            <item ref="p3"><m>p_3</m></item>
            <item ref="p2"><m>p_2</m></item>
            <item ref="p1"><m>p_1</m></item>
          </legend>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="fgraph"
              text="Schematic graph of f of x equals e to the x with its Taylor polynomials p 1 through p 4 about 0. Dashed markers show a representative positive x with an intermediate value c between 0 and x, and a representative negative x with an intermediate c between x and 0. The sample points show the size of e to the c used to bound the remainder">
            <annotation ref="fgraph" text="The function f of x equals e to the x, a thick blue curve" sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="p1" text="The first degree Taylor polynomial p 1"/>
            <annotation ref="p2" text="The second degree Taylor polynomial p 2"/>
            <annotation ref="p3" text="The third degree Taylor polynomial p 3"/>
            <annotation ref="p4" text="The fourth degree Taylor polynomial p 4"/>
            <annotation ref="center" text="The center of expansion where the curves meet on the vertical axis"/>
            <annotation ref="pos-x" text="A dashed marker at a representative positive x"/>
            <annotation ref="pos-sample" text="A point at c between 0 and the positive x, where e to the c is less than e to the x"/>
            <annotation ref="neg-x" text="A dashed marker at a representative negative x"/>
            <annotation ref="neg-sample" text="A point at c between the negative x and 0, where e to the c is less than 1"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>

    <p>
      The second case is when <m>x &lt; 0</m>. In this case, since <m>c</m> is between <m>x</m> and <m>0</m>,
      we have <m>x \leq c \leq 0</m>. Hence, we can bound the remainder term as follows:
    </p>
    <me>
      |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{1}{(n+1)!} |x|^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      Now we can compute the limit of the remainder term as <m>n\to \infty</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \lim_{n\to \infty} |R_n(x)| \leq \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{1}{(n+1)!} |x|^{n+1} = 0
    </me>
    <p>
      This shows that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x &lt; 0</m>. Therefore, 
      we conclude that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x\in \R</m>.
    </p>    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsection-3">
    <title> The error in using a Taylor polynomial</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will discuss how to approximate the error in using a Taylor polynomial to approximate a function. 
      We will use the remainder theorem to bound the error term <m>R_n(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose we want to approximate <m>f(x)</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=a</m>. 
      The error in this approximation is given by <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>. By the remainder theorem, we have:
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      To bound the error term, we need to find an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between 
      <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
    </p> 
    <example>
      <title>Approximating <m>e^x</m> using a Taylor polynomial</title>
      <p>
        Approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>. 
        Find an upper bound for the error in this approximation.
      </p>
      </example>
      <solution>
      <p>
        We will approximate <m>f(x) = e^x</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_2(x) = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2}
      </me>
      <p> 
        We want to approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m>. The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.5) = \frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(3)}(x) = e^x</m>, we have:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_2(0.5)| \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, the error in approximating <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 is bounded by:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_2(0.5)| \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.125) \approx 0.03435
      </me>
      <p>
      In fact, if we compute the actual value of <m>e^{0.5}</m> and compare it with the approximation
      given by <m>p_2(0.5)</m>, we find that the actual error is <m>e^{0.5} - p_2(0.5) \approx 0.02372</m>, which 
      is indeed less than the upper bound we calculated.
      </p>
       
  <interactive geogebra="xzdc5jpq" width="95%" shift-drag-zoom="no" aspect="16:11">
  <description>
    The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and
    the blue curve is the graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_2(x)</m>.
    The exact value of <m>e^{0.5}</m> is represented by the red point and
    the approximation given by <m>p_2(0.5)</m> is represented by the blue point.
    The vertical distance between the two points represents the error in the approximation.
    Note that the error is less than the upper bound we calculated, i.e., <m>0.03435</m>.
  </description>
  </interactive>
  <p>
  The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and
    the blue curve is the graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_2(x)</m>.
    The exact value of <m>e^{0.5}</m> is represented by the red point and
    the approximation given by <m>p_2(0.5)</m> is represented by the blue point.
    The vertical distance between the two points represents the error in the approximation.
    Note that the error is less than the upper bound we calculated, i.e., <m>0.03435</m>.
  </p>
  </solution>

    <example> 
      <title>Given a desired error bound, find values of <m>x</m> for which the approximation is guaranteed to be valid</title>
      <p>
        Assume that we use the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 at <m>x=0</m> to approximate <m>\sin(x)</m>. For approximately what values of <m>x</m> 
        can you replace <m>\sin(x)</m> by such a Taylor polynomial with an error of magnitude no greater than <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m>?
      </p>
      </example>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The Taylor polynomial of degree 3 at <m>x=0</m> for <m>\sin(x)</m> is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_3(x) = x - \frac{x^3}{6}
      </me>
      <p>
       Since <m>p_3(x) = p_4(x)</m>, the error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_4(x) = \frac{f^{(5)}(c)}{5!} x^5
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(5)}(x) = \cos(x)</m>, we have:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_4(x)| \leq \frac{|x|^5}{120}
      </me>
      <p>
        We want to find the values of <m>x</m> such that:
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{|x|^5}{120} \leq 4 \times 10^{-3}
      </me>
      <p>
        This gives us:
      </p>
      <me>
        |x|^5 \leq 0.48
      </me>
      <p>
        Taking the fifth root, we get:
      </p>
      <me>
        |x| \leq 0.86347
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we can replace <m>\sin(x)</m> by the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 
        at <m>x=0</m> with an error of magnitude no greater than 
        <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m> for <m>|x| \leq 0.86347</m>.
      </p>
    <interactive geogebra="ptgj7hpk" material-width="790" material-height="500" 
    shift-drag-zoom="no" aspect="16:10"/>
    <description>
     The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> 
     and the blue curve is the graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_3(x)= x - \frac{x^3}{6}</m>. 
     Use the slider to change the value of <m>x</m> and see how the error changes for different 
     values of <m>x</m>. Note that for the slider values have been limited to fit the page. 
     </description>

    <interactive geogebra="z854nrgd" material-width="736" material-height="423" shift-drag-zoom="no" aspect="16:9"/>
    <description>
     Use the slider to change the value of <m>x_0</m> and see how the error changes. 
     Note that for <m>|x_0| \leq 0.86347</m>, the error is less than <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m>.
    </description>
    </solution>
  
  
  <example>
    <title> Finding the value of <m>n</m> such that the error is less than a given tolerance</title>
    <p>
      Find the smallest value of <m>n</m> for which the polynomial approximation for <m>\sin(x)</m> 
      is accurate to <m>±0.005</m> for values of <m>x</m> in the interval <m>[-\pi/2, \pi/2]</m>.
      </p>
      </example>
      
    <solution>
      <p>
        The Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=0</m> for <m>\sin(x)</m> is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{(-1)^k}{(2k+1)!} x^{2k+1}
      </me>
      <p>
        The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(n+1)}(x)</m> is either <m>\cos(x)</m> or <m>\sin(x)</m>, we have:  
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}
      </me>
      <p>
        We want to find the smallest value of <m>n</m> such that:
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \leq 0.005
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>|x| \leq \pi/2</m> for <m>x \in [-\pi/2, \pi/2]</m>, and approximating 
        <m>\pi/2 \approx 2</m>, we have:
      </p>
        <me>
        \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \lt \frac{2^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}
        </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we want to find the smallest value of <m>n</m> such that:  
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{2^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \lt 0.005
      </me>
      <p>
        We can check the values of <m>n</m> starting from <m>n=0</m> until 
       we find the smallest value of <m>n</m> that satisfies the inequality. 
       After checking, we find that the smallest value of <m>n</m> that satisfies 
       the inequality is <m>n=8</m>. 
      </p>
      <interactive desmos="7b85e78f17" width="70%" aspect="1:2" />
      </solution> 
  
      
</subsection>

  
  <subsection>
    <title>Applications of Taylor Series and Remainder Theorem</title>
    <p>
      Physicists often use Taylor series to approximate functions in order to 
      simplify calculations. The first example is the kinetic energy of an object 
      in relativistic mechanics. The second example is the approximation 
      of the period of a pendulum. We will discuss the error in using these approximations and 
      when it is valid to use them. Additionally, we will discuss how to use the taylor series to 
      estimate value of <m>\pi</m> in the last example.
    </p>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Approximating Relativistic Kinetic Energy</title>
      <p>
        In relativistic mechanics, the mass of an object moving with velocity <m>v</m> is given by: 
        <me>
          m = \frac{m_0}{\sqrt{1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}}}
        </me>
        where <m>m_0</m> is the rest mass of the object and <m>c</m> is the speed of light.
        Then the kinetic energy of an object of mass <m>m</m> moving with velocity <m>v</m> is: 
      </p>
      <me>
        K = mc^2-m_0c^2
      </me>
      <p>
      In the case when <m>v \ll c</m>, we can use the Taylor series to approximate the kinetic energy. 
      In example <xref ref="example-1"/> we will show that the kinetic energy can be approximated by 
      the formula <m>K \approx \frac{1}{2} m_0v^2</m> when <m>v \ll c</m>. See <xref ref="fig-relativistic-ke"/> 
      for a comparison of the relativistic kinetic energy and its Newtonian approximation. 
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ke-cartoon">
  <caption>Relativistic versus Newtonian kinetic energy. The relativistic
  energy races toward a wall at the speed of light <m>c</m>, while the
  Newtonian energy follows a gentle parabola; the two agree only when
  <m>v \ll c</m>.</caption>
  <image width="65%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ke-cartoon">
      <diagram dimensions="(440,330)" margins="8">
        <!-- Cartoonish: relativistic KE races toward the "wall" at v=c;
             Newtonian KE ambles along a gentle parabola. Natural units c=1, m0=1. -->
        <definition>Krel(v) = 1/sqrt(1 - v^2) - 1</definition>
        <definition>Knewt(v) = v^2/2</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-0.08, -0.22, 1.2, 2.35)">
          <axes at="axes" xlabel="v" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
          <!-- the cosmic speed-limit "wall" at v = c, with hatching -->
          <group at="wall">
            <line p1="(1, 0)" p2="(1, 2.3)" stroke="gray" thickness="2.5"/>
            <repeat parameter="k=0..10">
              <line p1="(1, 0.02 + 0.21*k)" p2="(1.07, 0.13 + 0.21*k)"
                    stroke="gray" thickness="1.5"/>
            </repeat>
          </group>
          <!-- chunky curves -->
          <graph at="newtonian" function="Knewt" domain="(0, 1.16)"
                 stroke="green" thickness="4.5"/>
          <graph at="relativistic" function="Krel" domain="(0, 0.95)"
                 stroke="blue" thickness="4.5"/>
          <!-- axis landmarks -->
          <label at="c-label" anchor="(1, 0)" alignment="ne" offset="(9,3)"><m>c</m></label>
          <label at="origin-label" anchor="(0, 0)" alignment="sw"><m>0</m></label>
          <!-- playful direct labels -->
          <label at="rel-label" anchor="(0.86, Krel(0.86))" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,8)">
            <m>\text{relativistic}</m>
          </label>
          <label at="newt-label" anchor="(0.8, Knewt(0.8))" alignment="se" offset="(4,-6)">
            <m>\text{Newtonian}</m>
          </label>
          <!-- callout: the two agree when v is small -->
          <label at="agree-label" anchor="(0.28, 0.62)" alignment="n">
            <m>\text{For}\, v \ll c: K_{\text{rel}} \approx K_{\text{new}}</m>
          </label>
          <line at="agree-arrow" p1="(0.30, 0.55)" p2="(0.42, 0.14)"
                stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="relativistic"
              text="A cartoon comparison of relativistic and Newtonian kinetic energy versus speed v. The relativistic energy races upward toward a wall at the speed of light c, while the Newtonian energy ambles along a gentle parabola">
            <annotation ref="relativistic"
                text="The relativistic kinetic energy, which shoots up without bound as v approaches the speed of light c"
                sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="newtonian"
                text="The Newtonian kinetic energy, a gentle parabola that stays finite and matches the relativistic curve only when v is much less than c"
                sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="wall"
                text="A hatched wall at v equals c, the speed of light, a barrier the relativistic energy approaches but never reaches"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-relativistic-ke">
  <caption>Relativistic kinetic energy compared with its Newtonian
  approximation. The two nearly coincide when <m>v \ll c</m>, but the
  relativistic energy grows without bound as <m>v \to c</m>.</caption>
  <image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="relativistic-ke">
      <diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="8">
        <!-- Natural units: c = 1, m0 = 1.
             Relativistic:  Krel(v) = 1/sqrt(1 - v^2) - 1
             Newtonian:     Knewt(v) = (1/2) v^2 -->
        <definition>Krel(v) = 1/sqrt(1 - v^2) - 1</definition>
        <definition>Knewt(v) = v^2/2</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-0.05, -0.15, 1.15, 2.2)">
          <axes at="axes" xlabel="v" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
          <line at="asymptote" p1="(1, 0)" p2="(1, 2.2)" dash="5 5"
                stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
          <graph at="newtonian" function="Knewt" domain="(0, 1.12)"
                 stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
          <graph at="relativistic" function="Krel" domain="(0, 0.945)"
                 stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
          <label at="c-label" anchor="(1, 0)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,2)">
            <m>c</m>
          </label>
          <label at="origin-label" anchor="(0, 0)" alignment="sw">
            <m>0</m>
          </label>
          <legend at="legend" anchor="(0.06, 2.05)" alignment="se">
            <item ref="relativistic"><m>K_{rel} = mc^2 - m_0c^2</m></item>
            <item ref="newtonian"><m>K_{new} = \tfrac{1}{2}m_0v^2</m></item>
          </legend>
        </coordinates>
        <annotations>
          <annotation ref="relativistic"
              text="A comparison of relativistic and Newtonian kinetic energy as functions of speed v, for v between 0 and the speed of light c">
            <annotation ref="relativistic"
                text="The relativistic kinetic energy, K equals m c squared minus m naught c squared, which increases without bound as v approaches the speed of light c"
                sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="newtonian"
                text="The Newtonian approximation, K approximately one half m naught v squared, a parabola that closely matches the relativistic curve when v is much less than c"
                sonify="yes"/>
            <annotation ref="asymptote"
                text="A dashed vertical line at v equals c, the speed of light, which the relativistic energy approaches as a vertical asymptote"/>
          </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
</figure>
      <example xml:id="example-1">
        <title>Newtonian kinetic energy as an approximation to relativistic kinetic energy when <m>v \ll c</m></title>
        <p>
        Show that the kinetic energy of an object moving with velocity
        <m>v</m> can be approximated by the formula <m>K \approx \frac{1}{2} m_0v^2</m> when <m>v \ll c</m>.
        </p>
        <hint>
          <p>
            We can use the Taylor series for <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - x}}</m> at <m>x=0</m> to approximate <m>K</m>. 
            Use <m>x = \frac{v^2}{c^2}</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>
        </example>
        <solution>
          <p>
            We can rewrite the kinetic energy as follows:
          </p>
          <me>
            K = mc^2-m_0c^2 = m_0c^2 \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}}} - 1 \right)
          </me>
          <p>
            Let <m>x = \frac{v^2}{c^2}</m>. Then we have:
          </p>
          <me>
            K = m_0c^2 \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - x}} - 1 \right)
          </me> 
          <p>
            Now we can use the Taylor series for <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - x}}</m> at <m>x=0</m> 
            to approximate <m>K</m>. The Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m> is given by:
          </p>  

          <me>
            f(x) = 1 + \frac{1}{2}x + \frac{3}{8}x^2 + \frac{5}{16}x^3 + \cdots
          </me>
          <p>
            Therefore, we have:
          </p>
          <me>
            K = m_0c^2 \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - x}} - 1 \right) = m_0c^2 \left( \frac{1}{2}x + \frac{3}{8}x^2 + \frac{5}{16}x^3 + \cdots \right) 
          </me>
          <p>
            Substituting <m>x = \frac{v^2}{c^2}</m>, we get:
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> K \amp  = m_0c^2 \left( \frac{1}{2}\frac{v^2}{c^2} + \frac{3}{8}\frac{v^4}{c^4} + \frac{5}{16}\frac{v^6}{c^6} + \cdots \right) </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp  = \frac{1}{2} m_0v^2 + \frac{3}{8} m_0\frac{v^4}{c^2} + \frac{5}{16} m_0\frac{v^6}{c^4} + \cdots </mrow>
          </md>
          <p>
            When <m>v \ll c</m>, the higher order terms in the series become negligible, and we can approximate the kinetic energy as:
          </p>
          <me>
            K \approx \frac{1}{2} m_0v^2.
          </me>
        </solution>
      
      
      <example> 
        <title>Estimating the error in using the Newtonian kinetic energy formula to approximate the relativistic kinetic energy</title>
        <p>
        Assume that a car a moving with a velocity of <m>v = 30 m/s </m> (<m>\approx 67</m> miles per hour). Use the remainder's theorem 
        to estimate the error in using the Newtonian kinetic energy formula <m>K \approx \frac{1}{2} m_0v^2</m> to approximate 
        the relativistic kinetic energy of the car. The speed of light is <m>c = 3 \times 10^8 m/s</m>.
        </p>
      </example>

      

        <solution>
          <p>
            Note that when using the Newtonian kinetic energy formula, we are using
             the Taylor polynomial of degree 1 at <m>x=0</m> to approximate <m>K</m>.
            Therefore, the error term is given by:
          </p> 
          <me>
            R_1(x) = m_0 c^2 \frac{f^{(2)}(c)}{2!} x^2
          </me>
          <p>
            Since <m>f^{(2)}(x) = \frac{3}{4} (1-x)^{-5/2}</m>, we have:
          </p>
          <me>
            |R_1(x)| \leq m_0 c^2 \frac{3}{8} (1-x)^{-5/2} x^2
          </me>
          <p>
            Since <m>x = \frac{v^2}{c^2}</m>, we have:
          </p>
          <me>
            |R_1(x)| \leq m_0 c^2 \frac{3}{8} \left( 1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2} \right)^{-5/2} \left( \frac{v^2}{c^2} \right)^2
          </me>
          <p>
            Substituting <m>v/c = 10^{-7}</m> and  <m>c = 3 \times 10^8 m/s</m>, we get:
          </p>
          <me>
            |R_1(x)| \leq m_0 (3 \times 10^8)^2 \frac{3}{8} \left( 1 -  10^{-14} \right)^{-5/2} 
            \left(10^{-14} \right)^2 \lt 4 \times 10^{-11} m_0
          </me>
          <p>
            This shows that the error is extremely small, and the Newtonian kinetic energy formula 
            is a very good approximation to the relativistic kinetic energy for a car moving at <m> 30 m/s</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
   
      </subsubsection>


     <subsubsection>
    <title>Approximating the value of <m>\pi</m> using Taylor series</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will discuss how to approximate the value of <m>\pi</m> using Taylor series. 
      We will use the Taylor series for <m>\arctan(x)</m> to approximate <m>\pi</m>. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Approximating <m>\pi</m> using the Taylor series for <m>\arctan(x)</m></title>
      <p>
        Use the Taylor series for <m>\arctan(x)</m> to approximate the value of <m>\pi</m>. 
        Find an upper bound for the error in this approximation.
      </p>
    </example>
    </subsubsection>

